  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">APLMA is supporting Cambodia to plan for transition from donor financing as the country approaches malaria elimination.  In July 2022 APLMA, UCSF and the Government of Cambodia co-hosted a meeting to discuss Cambodia&#x2019;s malaria financing and transition planning. The meeting was opened by the Minister of Health and was attended by governors/vice-governors and health department directors from all malaria-endemic provinces, along with representatives from other ministries and development partners. Cambodia has now requested APLMA support for the development of a financing sustainability plan, with a focus on the prevention of malaria re-establishment and continuity for village-based malaria workers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">For countries that are close to malaria elimination but which border countries with a higher disease burden (such as Bhutan, Nepal, and Timor-Leste), establishing effective cross-border outbreak management and data-sharing mechanisms are critically important. Building on a high-level meeting hosted by the Government of Bhutan in December 2021, APLMA fostered policy dialogue on cross-border malaria elimination by supporting meetings between India and Nepal in April 2022, and India and Bhutan in June 2022. APLMA also supported a landscape assessment in Indonesia and Timor-Leste to develop recommendations that can be jointly adopted to improve management of border malaria.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023-24 Australia&#x2019;s support to the Asia Pacific Leaders Malaria Alliance helped to improve the quality of health services in partner countries through provision of training and professional development, and provision of technical assistance. This included:
&#x200d;
&#x2022;             identifying gaps in National Malaria Programs, and assisting in the development of action plans for surveillance and response;
&#x200d;
&#x2022;             conducting a four-day virtual Strategic Planning for Malaria Vector Surveillance and Control course in September 2023, with 236 participants from 29 countries, to assist in developing policy and operational guidelines;&#x200d;
&#x2022;             developing healthcare worker training materials, and delivering six technical webinars, to a total of 1,271 participants, to help build workforce capacity in the Indo-Pacific region.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Asia Pacific Leaders&#x2019; Malaria Alliance (APLMA) is a regional policy and advocacy platform representing governments from Asia and the Pacific who have committed to ending malaria by 2030. It was formed at the East Asia Summit at the initiative of co-chairs Australia and Vietnam. In 2024-2025 Australia&#x2019;s support to APLMA helped deliver the 9th Asia Pacific Leaders&#x2019; Summit on Malaria Elimination hosted by the Government of Indonesia. The two-day event, in June 2025 was a platform to reinforce commitments towards malaria elimination, exchange effective strategies, and rally support from partner nations and global institutions to accelerate initiatives in the region. In the past 12 months APLMA supports a range of activities to combat malaria and raise awareness across the region including; delivering malaria budget advocacy training in Cambodia, Vietnam and Indonesia; engaging the former Indonesian President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono as a Special Adviser and to undertake high-level advocacy; completing a baseline report on the intersection between Gender Equity, Disability and Social Inclusion (GEDSI) and malaria in the Asia Pacific region; and promoting awareness of malaria elimination through articles, blog posts and case studies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Asia Pacific Leaders&#x2019; Malaria Alliance (APLMA) is a regional policy and advocacy platform representing heads of government from Asia and the Pacific who have committed to ending malaria by 2030. It was formed at the East Asia Summit at the initiative of co-chairs Australia and Vietnam. In 2023-24 Australia&#x2019;s support to APLMA helped formalise its partnership with ASEAN based on selected projects aligned with the ASEAN Health Cluster 2 and 3 Workplans 2021-2025. APLMA shared a progress update report with the ASEAN Secretary General, focusing on significant regional epidemiological and policy developments over the past year. In response, the Secretary-General wrote to acknowledge APLMA&#x2019;s continued work in the region. This work helped to ensure the Chairman&#x2019;s Statement at the 43rd ASEAN Summit included a reference to malaria, a notable addition to the previous year&#x2019;s statement, which did not.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
